<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Soothing Guanabana</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; " colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze simple editorials or opinion pieces. Reading: Respond to questions based on class readings. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of basic syllabification rules when decoding two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze information about healing plants in Panama (e.g., "The text describes the most common healing plants of Panama."). Can respond to questions about the types and benefits of plants in class discussions (e.g., "What is mint for?" "It is good for the stomach."). Can apply basic syllabification rules to decode and accurately pronounce two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words, enhancing phonemic awareness (e.g., "treatment," "therapeutic").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of different parts of a soursop plant (fruit, leaves, root) and briefly reviews vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., *soursop*, *leaf*, *root*, *fruit*). The teacher asks students: "What is *guanabana* used for?" to prompt recall of general uses and connect to the theme. Students briefly share their ideas (e.g., "It's a delicious fruit," "Some people say it is good for health").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture of *guanabana* leaves. The teacher says: "These *leaves* are *used to treat* *infections*. Listen carefully: *in-fec-tions* (demonstrating syllabification). You *should* boil them first." The teacher models reading a short sentence related to *guanabana* uses, emphasizing clear pronunciation of target vocabulary like *treatment* and *therapeutic*, and showing how to stress the first syllable of compound nouns like "herbal garden" if applicable to the text.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are *guanabana leaves* good for *headaches* or *infections*?" (Expected answer: "Infections.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we eat the *leaves* directly, or *should* we *prepare* them?" (Expected answer: "We *should* *prepare* them.")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simplified editorial-style text or informative article titled "The Soothing Guanabana: A Natural Remedy". The text (approximately 100-120 words) describes various uses of *guanabana* (soursop) for common ailments, incorporating present simple facts, "used to" for purpose, and "should/shouldn't" for advice. It includes vocabulary such as *plant, leaf, root, remedy, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, medicinal, natural, therapeutic, boil, prepare, traditionally, safely*.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Analyze simple editorials or opinion pieces. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of basic syllabification rules.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text silently for the first time. The teacher asks: "What is the main idea of this article?" (Expected answer: "It's about how *guanabana* is a *natural remedy*.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Questions:</b> Students read the text again and answer 3-4 True/False statements projected on the board.
            <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Soursop leaves are used to treat infections." (True)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should eat the root of the soursop fruit." (False)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Guanabana is a useful medicinal plant." (True)</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Information:</b> Students match phrases about *guanabana* plant parts to their uses (e.g., "Soursop leaves are used to..." matches "...treat infections.").</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific details, using infinitive of purpose ("used to" + verb), giving advice ("should/shouldn't"), present simple for facts. Application of syllable division rules for decoding words like "treatment" and "therapeutic."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Reading:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the text, but with key verbs (e.g., *treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare*) and modal verbs (*should/shouldn't*) missing. They fill in the blanks, referencing the text if needed. Example: "Guanabana leaves are _______ to _______ *infections*." (used/treat). "You _______ *boil* the *leaves* *safely*." (should).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled - Syllabification and Pronunciation Focus:</b> The teacher writes a list of target two-syllable, three-syllable, or compound words from the text on the board (e.g., *treatment, therapeutic, traditionally, natural, application, medicinal, infections*). Students work individually to divide the words into syllables on their worksheets. The teacher then leads a choral repetition, calling on individual students to pronounce words and explain their syllable divisions. Example: Teacher writes "therapeutic," asks a student to pronounce, then another student to mark the syllables: "the-ra-peu-tic."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Question Answering & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students take turns asking and answering comprehension questions about specific details in the text. Student A asks, Student B answers, then they switch roles. They must use "used to" or "should/shouldn't" in their answers.
            <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the *guanabana plant* is *used to treat infections*?" (The *leaves* are *used to treat* *infections*.)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you want to use *guanabana* as a *remedy*, what *should* you do with the *leaves*?" (You *should boil* them *safely* to *prepare* a tea.)</li>
            </ul>
            Pairs also check each other's syllabification work from activity 2, discussing any differences.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Advice Exchange.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of "Symptom Cards" (e.g., "Minor headache," "Feeling tired," "Small burn," "Stomach discomfort," "Skin infection") and "Remedy Cards" with short descriptions of healing plants (not just *guanabana*, but also *aloe vera*, *lemongrass*, *mint leaves* from Lesson 1, ensuring the relevant vocabulary is recycled and extended) and their uses, similar to the main reading text structure. The task is to read each Symptom Card and then, as a group, select the most appropriate Remedy Card, explaining their choice based on the reading. They *should* use the target grammar (e.g., "For a *skin infection*, you *should* use *soursop leaves*. They are *used to treat* *infections*."). They *should* also mention how to *prepare* it. Teacher circulates, provides support, and monitors for correct use of language and syllabification in pronunciation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during Stage 4 and a short individual "Exit Ticket."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Observation Checklist:</b> Teacher assesses groups on their ability to: 1) correctly match remedies to symptoms based on text comprehension; 2) use "used to" and "should/shouldn't" correctly when giving advice; 3) pronounce target multi-syllable words from the text intelligibly.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short written task before leaving.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (for Exit Ticket):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of response to questions about the text (e.g., "What is *guanabana* *used to treat*?").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least one instance of "used to" or "should/shouldn't."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying syllables for one target word from the lesson (e.g., "Divide 'treatment' into syllables.").</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Write one sentence about a *healing plant* you read about today and what it is *used for*."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you in reading new *medicinal* words and dividing them into syllables?" (Scale of 1-3: 1=Not confident, 2=Okay, 3=Very confident)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting fact you learned about *The Soothing Guanabana* today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word you learned to pronounce, paying attention to its syllables?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., text comprehension, syllabification practice, group discussion)."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with analyzing the text or applying syllabification rules? (e.g., provide a glossary, slower reading pace, more modeling for pronunciation)."</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The vocabulary and understanding of *healing plants* (especially *guanabana*) will be directly applied in Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction). Students will use the information they read and practiced to discuss and exchange advice about *natural remedies* with partners, focusing on oral fluency and interaction.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>